1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,300
Welcome to Module Nineteen, the last module of the unit on niches and tools.

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:10,912
A bee landing on a flower it has never met before still knows, roughly, what to do.

3
00:00:10,912 --> 00:00:16,825
Over a very long time, flowers and pollinators have converged on shared cues.

4
00:00:16,825 --> 00:00:19,750
Nectar guides that glow in ultraviolet.

5
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:21,325
Landing platforms.

6
00:00:21,325 --> 00:00:24,412
Colours and scents that mean, reward here.

7
00:00:24,412 --> 00:00:32,800
Because the signal is standard, any compatible pollinator can work any compatible flower, and the whole network becomes richly connected.

8
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:39,262
The Model Context Protocol, usually just called M C P, is the agent world's shared cue.

9
00:00:39,262 --> 00:00:45,300
A service that offers an M C P server can be used by any agent that speaks M C P.

10
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:50,100
And the agent can ask any such server a simple question: what tools do you have?

11
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:54,512
That one listing step is what lets a new partner be useful right away.

12
00:00:54,512 --> 00:00:57,412
M C P is different from yesterday's skills.

13
00:00:57,412 --> 00:01:01,550
A skill is a folder of instructions the agent reads itself.

14
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:06,037
An M C P server is a separate running program that the agent talks to.

15
00:01:06,037 --> 00:01:07,737
They work well together.

16
00:01:07,737 --> 00:01:09,237
Now scale it up.

17
00:01:09,237 --> 00:01:16,500
Uber described what happened when hundreds of their teams started connecting agents to internal services, each in their own way.

18
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:21,862
Tools were scattered, duplicated and hard to find, and security was inconsistent.

19
00:01:21,862 --> 00:01:23,525
So they built a gateway.

20
00:01:23,525 --> 00:01:35,350
One place that keeps a catalogue of every server and tool, checks who is calling, removes personal data from responses, and translates agent calls into the language of each internal service.

21
00:01:35,350 --> 00:01:38,200
Two of their design choices are worth stealing.

22
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:40,925
First, discovery does not mean exposure.

23
00:01:40,925 --> 00:01:47,137
Automatically generated tools start switched off, until the owning team reviews and enables them.

24
00:01:47,137 --> 00:01:51,337
Second, with thousands of tools, you can't show them all to a model.

25
00:01:51,337 --> 00:01:57,462
So the agent searches for a server, then looks up its tools, then fetches only the one it needs.

26
00:01:57,462 --> 00:02:00,550
You've now met that same pattern three times.

27
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:02,650
It's called progressive disclosure.

28
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:05,062
You can also reshape the agent itself.

29
00:02:05,062 --> 00:02:09,650
Claude Code mods are small programs that watch everything happening in a session.

30
00:02:09,650 --> 00:02:17,650
They can observe an event, rewrite it, or answer it themselves, for example by refusing a dangerous command before it runs.

31
00:02:17,650 --> 00:02:20,287
Here's where the pollination story breaks.

32
00:02:20,287 --> 00:02:26,225
Mutualisms evolve slowly, and cheaters are kept in check by slow ecological feedback.

33
00:02:26,225 --> 00:02:34,962
An M C P server, written by a stranger, can join your agent in seconds, and whatever its tools say flows straight into your agent's context.

34
00:02:34,962 --> 00:02:36,662
Trust can't evolve here.

35
00:02:36,662 --> 00:02:38,762
You have to manage it on purpose.

36
00:02:38,762 --> 00:02:50,712
In the lab, you'll connect a free browser server to your capstone project, use it to read one public species page and pull out its taxonomy, and then build a guard that blocks destructive commands.

37
00:02:50,712 --> 00:02:54,975
Then you'll ask your agent to delete a folder, and watch the guard say no.

38
00:02:54,975 --> 00:02:58,937
Together with yesterday's skill, that's your build for this unit.

39
00:02:58,937 --> 00:03:01,212
Let's go and open some flowers.

